Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:05.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,By some estimates, half the nitrogen in your body was pulled from the air by a machine.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.69,0:00:06.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,Not a plant.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.80,0:00:07.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,A machine.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.25,0:00:10.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,The air is seventy-eight percent nitrogen.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.02,0:00:12.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,Every cell needs it.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.01,0:00:15.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,For most of history, we couldn't use a breath of it.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.17,0:00:18.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,Three men cracked that problem.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.29,0:00:20.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,Only two became famous.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.44,0:00:24.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the same chemistry that fed billions would also fuel a war.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.68,0:00:30.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,In 1898, the British scientist Sir William Crookes used his presidential address to
Dialogue: 0,0:00:30.55,0:00:33.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,the British Association to deliver a warning.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.94,0:00:37.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,The world, he said, was heading toward a wheat shortage.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:37.93,0:00:39.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,Populations were rising.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.86,0:00:41.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Farmland was wearing out.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:41.78,0:00:44.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the thing crops needed most was running low.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.00,0:00:47.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,That thing was nitrogen.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:47.45,0:00:49.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,Plants can't use nitrogen gas.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.97,0:00:54.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,Its two atoms are bound so tightly that only a few things in nature can pull them apart
Dialogue: 0,0:00:54.84,0:00:58.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,certain bacteria, and the violence of lightning.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.71,0:01:04.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,Everything else has to be recycled: manure, compost, the remains of living things.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:05.09,0:01:08.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,By the nineteenth century, recycling wasn't enough.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:08.52,0:01:11.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the world went looking for nitrogen that was already fixed
Dialogue: 0,0:01:11.68,0:01:14.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,and found it in two strange places.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:14.95,0:01:19.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first was bird droppings: centuries of seabird guano,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:19.27,0:01:23.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,piled on islands off the coast of Peru and shipped to Europe by the ton.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.19,0:01:28.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,The second was the Atacama Desert, one of the driest places on Earth,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.58,0:01:32.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,where vast beds of sodium nitrate lay just beneath the surface.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.90,0:01:37.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,But in the 1870s, the richest of those deposits were not in Chile.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:37.56,0:01:40.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,They lay in Peru's southern province of Tarapacá,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.81,0:01:44.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,and along Bolivia's coast, around the port of Antofagasta.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.67,0:01:48.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,Much of the work there was done by Chilean and British companies.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:48.43,0:01:54.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,In 1878, Bolivia tried to raise taxes on the Chilean-run Antofagasta Nitrate Company.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:54.92,0:01:57.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,When Bolivia threatened to seize the company's property,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.79,0:02:00.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,Chilean forces occupied Antofagasta on February fourteenth,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:00.54,0:02:01.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,eighteen seventy-nine.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:03.57,0:02:05.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,Bolivia declared war on Chile
Dialogue: 0,0:02:05.48,0:02:10.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,and Chile went to war with both Bolivia and its ally, Peru.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:10.29,0:02:13.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,It became known as the War of the Pacific.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:13.22,0:02:15.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,It lasted five years.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:15.30,0:02:16.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,Chile won.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:16.82,0:02:21.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,In the Treaty of Ancón, in 1883, Peru gave up Tarapacá.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:21.73,0:02:25.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,A truce in 1884 gave Chile the entire Bolivian coast,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:25.63,0:02:29.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,with its nitrate, copper and mineral industries.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:29.56,0:02:32.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,A treaty in 1904 made it permanent.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:32.53,0:02:34.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,Bolivia has had no coastline... ever since.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:35.87,0:02:40.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,Chilean saltpeter became one of the world's most sought-after commodities.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:40.20,0:02:41.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,And here's the problem.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:41.53,0:02:43.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Nitrate didn't only grow wheat.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.77,0:02:46.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,It also made gunpowder and explosives.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:46.70,0:02:50.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,Whoever controlled those sea lanes controlled both food and firepower.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:50.60,0:02:55.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,Crookes's message was simple: the deposits would not last.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:55.51,0:02:58.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,The way forward was to take nitrogen straight from the air.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:59.08,0:03:01.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Someone had to figure out how.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:01.36,0:03:05.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,Fritz Haber was a chemist at the technical university in Karlsruhe,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:05.13,0:03:09.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Germany — ambitious, brilliant, and restless.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:09.77,0:03:12.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,He had begun studying the reaction that mattered:
Dialogue: 0,0:03:12.62,0:03:15.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,nitrogen plus hydrogen, making ammonia.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:16.56,0:03:18.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Haber had grown up in Breslau,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:18.51,0:03:22.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,then a German city, today Wrocław in Poland,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:22.31,0:03:23.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,in a Jewish family.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:23.96,0:03:28.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,He was proud, driven, and fiercely loyal to Germany.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:28.68,0:03:31.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,In 1901, he married another chemist from Breslau: Clara Immerwahr.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:33.97,0:03:37.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,Just months earlier, in December 1900,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:37.03,0:03:41.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,Clara had become the first woman to earn a doctorate from the University of Breslau.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:41.93,0:03:44.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,She defended her thesis in the university's main hall
Dialogue: 0,0:03:44.62,0:03:47.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,and many young women of the city came to watch.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:48.53,0:03:53.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,After the wedding, the expectations of the time left her little room for her own research.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.85,0:03:56.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,Her husband's career came first.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:56.59,0:03:57.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,Remember her name.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:58.29,0:04:01.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,In Part 2, she becomes part of the story.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:01.68,0:04:04.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,On paper, Haber's reaction is simple.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:04.64,0:04:06.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,In practice, it's a trap.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:07.11,0:04:11.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,Heat the gases, and the reaction speeds up — but the ammonia falls apart again.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:12.32,0:04:15.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Cool them down, and almost nothing happens at all.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:16.11,0:04:19.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Haber's early measurements found only tiny traces of ammonia.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:20.34,0:04:23.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,And in May 1907, at a meeting of the Bunsen Society in Hamburg,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:23.56,0:04:28.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,Walther Nernst — one of the most respected chemists in Germany
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.26,0:04:30.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,publicly called Haber's numbers very inaccurate.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:30.75,0:04:32.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,Haber was deeply offended.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:35.13,0:04:38.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,But Nernst had also proposed repeating the measurements at higher pressure.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:38.62,0:04:43.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,Haber took that idea further than anyone had dared.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:43.33,0:04:47.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,To do it, he needed someone who could build equipment that didn't yet exist.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:48.37,0:04:53.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,That person was Robert Le Rossignol — a young British chemist from the island of Jersey,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:53.85,0:04:57.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,trained at University College London, who had joined Haber in Karlsruhe.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:57.67,0:05:01.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Le Rossignol built the high-pressure apparatus
Dialogue: 0,0:05:01.19,0:05:06.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,with valves and seals that could hold gas at around two hundred times normal
Dialogue: 0,0:05:06.01,0:05:07.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,atmospheric pressure.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:08.17,0:05:10.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,Near the end of his life, he summed up their partnership simply:
Dialogue: 0,0:05:10.94,0:05:15.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,Haber did the theoretical side, and he did the engineering side.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:16.40,0:05:20.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,Their apparatus kept circulating the unreacted gas over a catalyst,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:20.69,0:05:22.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,drawing off ammonia as it formed.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:23.12,0:05:25.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,The rare metal osmium made it work.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:25.99,0:05:30.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,By the summer of 1909, they were ready to show it to the people who could make it real.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:31.10,0:05:35.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Since 1908, Haber had been working under contract with BASF
Dialogue: 0,0:05:35.51,0:05:42.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,the Badische Anilin- und Soda-Fabrik, one of the largest chemical companies in the world.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:42.46,0:05:49.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,On July 2nd, 1909, Haber and Le Rossignol invited BASF to Karlsruhe for a demonstration.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:50.08,0:05:51.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,Carl Bosch was invited.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:51.99,0:05:56.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,But it was the chemist Alwin Mittasch and the company's chief mechanic,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:56.03,0:05:57.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,Julius Kranz, who watched.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:58.05,0:06:01.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,What they saw was a machine that sat on a table.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:01.48,0:06:06.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,And out of it, drop by drop, came liquid ammonia — made from the air.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:06.99,0:06:10.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Roughly two cubic centimeters a minute, hour after hour.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:11.15,0:06:12.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,It wasn't much.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:12.38,0:06:14.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,But it was continuous.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:14.11,0:06:14.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,It didn't stop.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:14.67,0:06:19.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,Le Rossignol kept a sealed glass tube of the ammonia made that day.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:19.92,0:06:23.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,More than forty years later, he gave it to the Science Museum in London.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:24.19,0:06:29.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,The next day, Haber sent BASF's directors a report of what they had witnessed.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:29.24,0:06:32.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,But a machine on a table is not a factory.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.27,0:06:37.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,And there was a question that still hangs over this moment: whose invention was it?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:37.19,0:06:40.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,The process would be called the Haber process.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:40.38,0:06:42.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,Later, the Haber-Bosch process.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:43.03,0:06:45.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,But look closely... at the paperwork.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:46.07,0:06:50.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,On the American patent, Haber put Le Rossignol's name beside his own
Dialogue: 0,0:06:50.05,0:06:53.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,and gave him a share of the royalties that would one day make him rich.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:53.86,0:06:59.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,Le Rossignol later said that Haber had handled the whole patent matter.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:59.39,0:07:02.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,He left Karlsruhe in August 1909,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:02.32,0:07:06.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,just as BASF began the long work of industrialization.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:06.71,0:07:09.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,He went on to a career in industrial research in Britain,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:09.86,0:07:12.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,and used his royalty income for philanthropy.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:12.81,0:07:15.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,The Nobel Prize, when it came, went to Haber alone.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:15.70,0:07:19.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,Le Rossignol's name faded from the story.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:19.90,0:07:21.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here's a question for you.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:21.84,0:07:25.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,Who deserves the credit for an invention — the person with the idea,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:25.86,0:07:31.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,the person who builds the first working machine, or the person who makes it work for millions?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:31.29,0:07:33.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,Tell us what you think in the comments.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:33.50,0:07:39.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the second name on the process belonged to a man who hadn't built the tabletop machine at all.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:39.11,0:07:43.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,He was the one who had to make it work at a scale no one had ever attempted.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:43.27,0:07:48.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,Carl Bosch was a BASF chemist with an engineer's mind and training in metallurgy.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:48.74,0:07:56.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,In 1909, BASF gave him the job of turning a laboratory experiment into an industry.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:56.74,0:08:02.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,The pressures and temperatures it required went well beyond anything industry had handled before.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:02.74,0:08:04.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,Three problems stood in the way.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:04.91,0:08:06.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first was the catalyst.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:07.06,0:08:10.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,Osmium worked — but it was extremely rare.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:10.19,0:08:12.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,There wasn't enough of it to supply an industry.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:12.89,0:08:17.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the BASF laboratory under Alwin Mittasch began a systematic search
Dialogue: 0,0:08:17.88,0:08:21.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,testing one material after another in small test reactors.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:21.74,0:08:24.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,Bosch later said that, over the years,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:24.82,0:08:27.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,the number of tests grew to... twenty thousand.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:29.39,0:08:32.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,The answer was a catalyst based on iron,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:32.14,0:08:34.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,with small additions of other compounds:
Dialogue: 0,0:08:34.53,0:08:36.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,cheap, abundant, and durable.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:37.13,0:08:39.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,Iron-based catalysts are still used today.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:40.32,0:08:42.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,The second problem was more dangerous.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:42.85,0:08:47.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,Bosch's team had ordered strong steel reactor tubes from the Mannesmann works.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:47.59,0:08:51.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first two ran for eighty hours... Then they burst.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:52.16,0:08:56.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,When Bosch examined the metal, the inner wall had lost all its elasticity.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:56.84,0:08:59.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,As he explained in his Nobel lecture,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:59.03,0:09:02.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,hydrogen had penetrated the steel and turned it brittle.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:02.59,0:09:06.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Bosch, who knew metallurgy, found the mechanism: the hot,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:06.30,0:09:10.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,high-pressure hydrogen was pulling carbon out of the steel,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:10.04,0:09:12.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,leaving it soft and brittle at the same time.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:12.96,0:09:14.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,His solution was elegant.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:14.92,0:09:21.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,A thin lining of soft steel, inside a strong outer steel jacket drilled with many small holes.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:21.55,0:09:25.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hydrogen that seeped through the lining could escape through the holes before it
Dialogue: 0,0:09:25.08,0:09:26.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,weakened the jacket.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:26.51,0:09:28.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,The lining could simply be replaced.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:28.86,0:09:34.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,The third problem was everything else: making hydrogen and nitrogen cheaply and pure enough,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:34.72,0:09:38.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,compressing them, and building equipment that could run around the clock.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:38.62,0:09:41.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hydrogen alone required a new industry.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:41.56,0:09:45.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,BASF would make it from coal, coke, air and water
Dialogue: 0,0:09:45.14,0:09:50.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,in what Bosch later called the largest gas production facilities ever built.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:50.45,0:09:53.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,It took years and enormous risk.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:53.12,0:09:57.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,But Bosch's team made high-pressure chemistry work at industrial scale.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:57.66,0:10:02.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,That achievement would later earn Bosch his own Nobel Prize, in 1931.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:02.54,0:10:08.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,On September 9th, 1913, the world's first ammonia synthesis plant went into
Dialogue: 0,0:10:08.33,0:10:12.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,operation at Oppau — today part of Ludwigshafen, on the Rhine.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:13.55,0:10:19.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,According to BASF, the new plant could produce up to... thirty tonnes of ammonia a day.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:20.37,0:10:25.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,Nobody yet knew exactly how farmers' crops would respond to this new fertilizer.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:25.72,0:10:29.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in the spring of 1914, on Bosch's proposal,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:29.72,0:10:34.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,BASF set up an agricultural research station at Limburgerhof,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:34.20,0:10:36.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,near Ludwigshafen, to find out.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:36.88,0:10:41.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,What started as drops on a tabletop became one of the most important industrial
Dialogue: 0,0:10:41.89,0:10:43.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,processes ever created.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:44.07,0:10:48.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,Synthetic fertilizer spread across the world through the twentieth century,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:48.09,0:10:51.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,helping farmers grow far more food on the same land.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:51.97,0:10:55.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,Researchers estimate that by the early twenty-first century,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:55.26,0:10:59.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,close to... half of the world's population was being fed by food grown with nitrogen
Dialogue: 0,0:10:59.96,0:11:00.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,from this process.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:01.01,0:11:05.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Crookes's famine never came the way he feared.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:05.68,0:11:08.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,The chemists had pulled bread out of the air.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:08.76,0:11:11.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,But remember what else nitrate made.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:11.42,0:11:14.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,Germany had no nitrate deposits of its own.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:14.82,0:11:17.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,Most of what it used — for its fields,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:17.12,0:11:20.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,and for its explosives — came by sea, from Chile.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:21.48,0:11:25.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,In the summer of 1914, Europe went to war.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:25.52,0:11:27.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the sea lanes were about to close.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:28.23,0:11:32.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,Robert Le Rossignol, the British engineer who had built the first machine,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:32.04,0:11:33.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,was still living in Germany.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:34.29,0:11:37.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,When war broke out, he was interned as an enemy alien
Dialogue: 0,0:11:37.36,0:11:41.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,and later released to work for a German company for the rest of the war.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:42.17,0:11:43.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,In Part 2:
Dialogue: 0,0:11:43.41,0:11:46.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,how the process that fed the world kept an army fighting —
Dialogue: 0,0:11:47.12,0:11:51.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,and why Fritz Haber was standing behind a cloud of poison gas at Ypres.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:00.00,0:00:05.57,OST,,0,0,0,,{\fad(200,200)}≈ HALF OF YOUR BODY'S NITROGEN · ESTIMATE
